{"answer":"In our small community we keep everything simple and transparent. The pot will be held by the village elder, who is already trusted to keep records. Each time a fisher deposits a fine, the elder will write it down in the communal ledger that is displayed at the weighing station for all to see. The fine will be added to the pot and held until the village decides how best to use it – usually for shared needs such as food for the sick, tools for boats, or for communal celebrations. If the pot ever runs low, we’ll hold a quick meeting to decide whether to borrow from the pot or ask for extra contributions. This way everyone knows exactly who owns the pot, how the money is tracked, and what the money is for.

Restated proposal (unchanged):
Each fisher shall use only a single net per trip and cease fishing once they have captured 1 unit of fish, where one unit is one medium fish weighing approximately 1 kg. After each trip, the fisher brings the net to the communal weighing station. The elder places the fish on the shared balance. If the weight exceeds 1 kg, the fisher immediately gives 10 % of the excess weight to the community pot as a fine. The fisher then stops fishing and returns home.", "revised_policy":"Each fisher shall use only a single net per trip and cease fishing once they have captured 1 unit of fish, where one unit is one medium fish weighing approximately 1 kg. After each trip, the fisher brings the net to the communal weighing station. The elder places the fish on the shared balance. If the weight exceeds 1 kg, the fisher immediately gives 10 % of the excess weight to the community pot as a fine. The fisher then stops fishing and returns home.", "revised_operationalization":"The community pot will be held by the village elder, with contributions recorded in a communal ledger displayed at the weighing station. Fines will be deposited into the pot and used for shared community needs such as food for the sick, boat repairs, or communal celebrations. The pot’s balance will be reviewed at monthly meetings to decide on its use or if additional contributions are required.", "reasoning":"I kept the policy unchanged because the question only addressed the handling of the fine, not the fishing rule itself. I added details about who holds the pot, how it is tracked, and its purpose to answer the question fully while staying true to Beti’s pragmatic, community‑focused mindset."}

